---
// The specimen dossier: the six invented brands/artefacts the lecture
// content keeps citing by name across multiple weeks, rather than
// re-inventing a fresh throwaway example every time a lecture needs one.
// This is deliberately distinct from "the specimen shelf" named in weeks
// 1-2 (the physical stack of found footage students bring in and annotate)
// — the dossier below is the course's own recurring cast, verified against
// every lecture that names them (see the grep this page's PROCESS.md entry
// cites). No fact below is invented for this page; every sentence traces to
// a specific lecture case study, cross-linked at the end of each entry.
//
// Each artefact gets one hand-authored SVG "still" in the site's existing
// risograph/glitch idiom (see SlopDivider.astro, TellIcon.astro): a shared
// perforated film-frame border, currentColor line art, one
// var(--at-secondary, currentColor) accent per still marking the specific
// defect the case study names, and .slop-displace for the same turbulence
// filter used everywhere else. Decorative only — the prose beside each
// still already carries the information — so every still is aria-hidden.
//
// Each still is now paired with one real photograph (Unsplash/Pexels,
// downloaded into src/assets and run through astro:assets) of the genuine
// thing the invented artefact is parodying — a real dental office, a real
// small film set, a real unboxing. The pairing is the point: a real photo
// of a real thing sitting next to an invented artefact demonstrates the
// course's real-vs-fake argument instead of just asserting it.
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import SlopFilters from "../../components/SlopFilters.astro";
import TellReveal from "../../components/TellReveal.astro";
import "../../styles/slop-effects.css";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";
import radiantSmilePhoto from "../../assets/images/specimens/radiant-smile-dental.jpg";
import brightAperturePhoto from "../../assets/images/specimens/bright-aperture.jpg";
import everlinePhoto from "../../assets/images/specimens/everline-gadget-reviews.jpg";
import halcyonPhoto from "../../assets/images/specimens/halcyon-devotional-renders.jpg";
import nightshadePhoto from "../../assets/images/specimens/nightshade-and-nectar.jpg";
import marlowePhoto from "../../assets/images/specimens/marlowe-vance.jpg";

// Shared film-strip perforations, reused identically across all six stills
// so the family resemblance is structural, not incidental — the one thing
// every specimen still has in common is that it was pulled from a frame.
const perfXs = [10, 26, 42, 58, 74, 90, 106, 122, 138];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Specimen dossier"
  description="Six invented artefacts the lecture content keeps citing by name, illustrated and cross-referenced against the weeks that established them."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <SlopFilters />

  <p>
    A course arguing slop is a genre needs recurring examples, the way a
    film-studies unit on melodrama keeps returning to the same handful of
    stills instead of hunting down a fresh one every week. These six are the
    recurring cast: each invented, each turning up by name in at least two
    pieces of course content, none embellished past what its originating
    lecture already established. Read this as a reference shelf, not new
    material. A photograph sits beside each invented still for contrast, not
    decoration — the real thing on the left, the specimen it never was on the
    right. If a detail below surprises you, either the lecture it links to is
    wrong or this page is, and either way, say something.
  </p>

  <div class="specimens">
    <article class="specimen">
      <div class="specimen-media">
        <svg
          class="specimen-still slop-displace"
          viewBox="0 0 160 100"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <rect x="1" y="1" width="158" height="98" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="5" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="91" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {/* An overcorrected smile: too many, too-even teeth (Tell #6) */}
          <path d="M32 52 Q80 76 128 52" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
          <path d="M36 56 Q80 72 124 56" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
          {Array.from({ length: 11 }).map((_, i) => {
            const x = 38 + i * 8;
            return <path d={`M${x} 56.5 L${x} 71.5`} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" />;
          })}
          {/* The logo, resolving to a plausible non-word by the closing shot (Tell #7) */}
          <rect x="106" y="14" width="42" height="20" rx="3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
          <path
            d="M110 26 Q116 20 122 26 T134 26 T144 23"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--at-secondary, currentColor)"
            stroke-width="1.4"
          />
        </svg>
        <figure class="specimen-photo">
          <Image
            src={radiantSmilePhoto}
            alt="A bright, empty modern dental clinic room with a reclined dental chair and overhead exam light, no patients or staff present."
            width={480}
            height={720}
          />
          <figcaption>A real dental office. Radiant Smile Dental Co. is not one.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <h2>Radiant Smile Dental Co.</h2>
      <p class="specimen-tag">
        Illustrates Tell #6 (the Overcorrected Smile) and Tell #7 (Temporal
        Identity Drift) —{" "}
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-02/")}>Week 2, The anatomy of a tell</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Radiant Smile Dental Co. sells a thirty-second spot for a clinic that,
        as far as anyone has established, does not exist at the address
        given. Nobody built a waiting room for it. The clinic's own logo
        resolves correctly in the opening shot and drifts by the closing shot
        into a plausible four-letter shape that isn't a word, every patient in
        the before/after montage wears Tell #6's smile, and the narrator
        delivers "same-day appointments available" in the exact
        rising-then-falling pitch it used two lines earlier for "financing
        options" — the model has no way of knowing one of those clauses
        matters more to a listener than the other.
      </p>
      <TellReveal summary="What's the tell?">
        <p>
          Not any one of the three alone — a distracted scroller would let
          each pass. Stack the drifting logo, the overcorrected smile, and
          the pitch that can't distinguish a promise from a price list, and
          that stacking is the entire reason this ad made the shelf.
        </p>
      </TellReveal>
    </article>

    <article class="specimen">
      <div class="specimen-media">
        <svg
          class="specimen-still slop-displace"
          viewBox="0 0 160 100"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <rect x="1" y="1" width="158" height="98" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="5" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="91" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {/* Six fixed shots: wide, two singles, two close-ups, one insert */}
          {[0, 1, 2].map((col) =>
            [0, 1].map((row) => (
              <rect
                x={16 + col * 44}
                y={18 + row * 34}
                width="34"
                height="24"
                rx="2"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.4"
              />
            )),
          )}
          {/* A ring, continuous in the single, relocated to the wrong hand in the insert */}
          <circle cx="30" cy="60" r="2.4" fill="currentColor" />
          <circle cx="130" cy="42" r="2.4" fill="var(--at-secondary, currentColor)" />
        </svg>
        <figure class="specimen-photo">
          <Image
            src={brightAperturePhoto}
            alt="A small film crew operating a camera on a tripod alongside a lighting rig inside a cozy, real indoor film set."
            width={480}
            height={720}
          />
          <figcaption>A real crew, on a real set, keeping continuity by hand. Bright Aperture keeps it by copy-paste.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <h2>Bright Aperture</h2>
      <p class="specimen-tag">
        Illustrates coverage as the concept that breaks —{" "}
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-03/")}>Week 3, Prompting as direction</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Bright Aperture is a small production outfit with a method instead of
        a philosophy: generate every scene as exactly six fixed prompts —
        establishing wide, two singles, two close-ups, one insert — and
        copy-paste every continuity detail, wardrobe down to time of day,
        verbatim across all six rather than trust the model to remember what
        it was just told. It's a workaround for the medium's lack of memory,
        not a fix for it. Mostly, it works, which is the interesting part.
      </p>
      <TellReveal summary="What's the tell?">
        <p>
          A ring, present and correct in the single, relocated to the wrong
          hand by the time the insert shot renders — in roughly one take in
          five, by Bright Aperture's own account. Far fewer errors than a crew
          that varies its wording shot to shot, but not zero, because
          "coverage" assumes a continuity this medium doesn't structurally
          have yet.
        </p>
      </TellReveal>
    </article>

    <article class="specimen">
      <div class="specimen-media">
        <svg
          class="specimen-still slop-displace"
          viewBox="0 0 160 100"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <rect x="1" y="1" width="158" height="98" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="5" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="91" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {/* A box, open, never quite sealed at the flaps */}
          <path
            d="M50 40 L110 40 L110 78 L50 78 Z M50 40 L38 30 L122 30 L110 40 M38 30 L38 24 M122 30 L122 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.6"
            stroke-dasharray="4 3"
          />
          {/* A play triangle, standing in for a video that documents no real unboxing */}
          <path d="M72 50 L72 68 L90 59 Z" fill="var(--at-secondary, currentColor)" />
          <text x="80" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">$0.02</text>
        </svg>
        <figure class="specimen-photo">
          <Image
            src={everlinePhoto}
            alt="A person's hands lifting a smartphone out of its retail box on a table, mid-unboxing."
            width={480}
            height={720}
          />
          <figcaption>Someone actually unboxing a phone. Everline's version skips the someone.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <h2>Everline Gadget Reviews</h2>
      <p class="specimen-tag">
        Illustrates the economics of volume —{" "}
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-04/")}>Week 4, The economics of volume</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Everline Gadget Reviews posts AI-generated "unboxing" and demo videos
        for gadgets that, as far as anyone has checked, nobody at Everline has
        ever physically unboxed. Ninety seconds a video, six videos a day,
        every day.
      </p>
      <TellReveal summary="What's the economics?">
        <p>
          Each video costs, by a rough specimen-shelf estimate, under two
          dollars in generation compute. A short-form ad rate needs on the
          order of thirty to fifty thousand views to recoup that — cheap
          enough that a single video clearing half a million views once a
          month covers a month's entire output outright.
        </p>
      </TellReveal>
    </article>

    <article class="specimen">
      <div class="specimen-media">
        <svg
          class="specimen-still slop-displace"
          viewBox="0 0 160 100"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <rect x="1" y="1" width="158" height="98" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="5" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="91" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {/* An amber sunset that never varies, over a caption ticker */}
          <circle cx="80" cy="38" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" />
          <path d="M18 52 L142 52" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" />
          <rect x="30" y="62" width="100" height="5" rx="2.5" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.45" />
          {/* The line attributed to the wrong book, eleven thousand times */}
          <rect x="30" y="72" width="70" height="5" rx="2.5" fill="var(--at-secondary, currentColor)" />
          <rect x="30" y="82" width="88" height="5" rx="2.5" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.45" />
        </svg>
        <figure class="specimen-photo">
          <Image
            src={halcyonPhoto}
            alt="A silhouetted figure sitting quietly by a calm lake at sunrise, alone, no camera crew or screen in sight."
            width={480}
            height={720}
          />
          <figcaption>A real sunrise, watched by a real person, once. Halcyon renders eleven an hour.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <h2>Halcyon Devotional Renders</h2>
      <p class="specimen-tag">
        Illustrates the pipeline as unit of analysis and the economics that
        make it rational —{" "}
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-07/")}>Week 7, Systems thinking for slop</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-09/")}>Week 9, The business case for slop</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Halcyon posts eleven "quiet reflection" shorts an hour, across four
        accounts, out of a four-stage pipeline: a scraper pulls a verse and a
        mood tag, an LLM writes a sixty-word reflection, a TTS pass reads it
        over stock b-roll, and a moderator glances at a thumbnail grid before
        the batch goes out. That fourth stage is the whole of Halcyon's
        editorial judgement, and it was built to catch an extra finger in the
        b-roll — which is exactly the wrong thing to be watching for. In
        March, a batch attributed a verse to the wrong book eleven thousand
        times before anyone caught it, and even then it wasn't the reflection
        itself that got noticed; it was the correction thread outperforming
        the original post.
      </p>
      <p>
        Run the arithmetic Week 9 hands you and the incentive comes into
        focus: a render costs roughly forty cents of GPU time, the cull rate
        keeps one render in twelve, and the four accounts post around eighty
        survivors a day — a thousand renders and about four hundred dollars,
        daily, to get there. Halcyon's accounts each sit in the high
        hundred-thousands of followers. The arithmetic works. It just works
        by volume, not by care, and only for as long as generation cost keeps
        falling faster than ad rates do.
      </p>
      <TellReveal summary="What's the tell?">
        <p>
          Nothing in a single clip — that's the point. Halcyon's failure is
          pipeline-shaped, not frame-shaped, and a moderator scanning
          thumbnails for extra fingers will never once glance at a citation.
        </p>
      </TellReveal>
    </article>

    <article class="specimen">
      <div class="specimen-media">
        <svg
          class="specimen-still slop-displace"
          viewBox="0 0 160 100"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <rect x="1" y="1" width="158" height="98" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="5" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="91" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {/* Episode one's locked reference face */}
          <path
            d="M54 70 Q50 40 66 32 Q80 26 88 36 Q94 44 90 56 Q98 62 92 70 Q86 78 70 78 Q58 78 54 70 Z"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.6"
          />
          {/* Episode nine's drifted face, offset and dashed */}
          <path
            d="M62 74 Q56 46 74 36 Q88 30 96 42 Q102 50 96 60 Q104 66 96 74 Q88 82 74 82 Q64 82 62 74 Z"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--at-secondary, currentColor)"
            stroke-width="1.4"
            stroke-dasharray="3 3"
            opacity="0.75"
          />
          <path d="M18 88 L142 88" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.4" />
        </svg>
        <figure class="specimen-photo">
          <Image
            src={nightshadePhoto}
            alt="A couple embracing beside a lake at golden hour, warm backlight, the same two faces they had a moment ago."
            width={480}
            height={720}
          />
          <figcaption>Two real people, one real evening. No seed to drift.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <h2>Nightshade &amp; Nectar</h2>
      <p class="specimen-tag">
        Illustrates seed drift —{" "}
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-10/")}>Week 10, Sustained coherence</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Nightshade &amp; Nectar is a supernatural-romance serial, two minutes
        twice a week, and its whole visual-language document — as far as it's
        inferable from nine episodes of output — appears to run to three
        rules: amber light, a slow zoom, and the line "I've been waiting,"
        delivered in the same too-smooth cadence every single time. Three
        rules is thin. Thin enough that by episode nine, the lead's face has
        drifted far enough from episode one's locked reference that long-time
        viewers comment on it before the plot does — and nobody changed the
        seed on purpose.
      </p>
      <TellReveal summary="What's the tell?">
        <p>
          Seed drift: the sustained-coherence version of the six-fingered
          hand. Not one bad frame, but a slow divergence no single-clip
          critique would ever catch, because a single-clip critique only ever
          watches one clip. The bible never specified the face, so a drifted
          one still counts as on-brand.
        </p>
      </TellReveal>
    </article>

    <article class="specimen">
      <div class="specimen-media">
        <svg
          class="specimen-still slop-displace"
          viewBox="0 0 160 100"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <rect x="1" y="1" width="158" height="98" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="5" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {perfXs.map((x) => <rect x={x} y="91" width="6" height="4" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5" />)}
          {/* A credited performer, bust and shoulders */}
          <circle cx="80" cy="34" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" />
          <path d="M56 66 Q80 46 104 66 L104 74 L56 74 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" />
          {/* Three sequential blink frames, snapping shut a beat off cadence */}
          <rect x="18" y="82" width="18" height="12" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.1" />
          <rect x="40" y="82" width="18" height="12" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.1" />
          <rect x="62" y="82" width="18" height="12" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.1" />
          <ellipse cx="27" cy="88" rx="3.4" ry="2.2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" />
          <path d="M43 88 L55 88" stroke="var(--at-secondary, currentColor)" stroke-width="1.4" />
          <ellipse cx="71" cy="88" rx="3.4" ry="2.2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" />
          <path d="M96 84 L138 84 M96 90 L138 90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.5" />
        </svg>
        <figure class="specimen-photo">
          <Image
            src={marlowePhoto}
            alt="A moody black-and-white studio portrait of a man in quiet contemplation, dramatic side lighting."
            width={480}
            height={720}
          />
          <figcaption>A real face, held by a real actor, for exactly as long as the shutter was open.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <h2>Marlowe Vance</h2>
      <p class="specimen-tag">
        Illustrates synthetic-performer disclosure —{" "}
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-11/")}>Week 11, The director's statement</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Marlowe Vance has led several unrelated slop productions over the
        past two years without once being a person. The voice-and-likeness
        model behind the name has never had its training data fully
        disclosed, and is widely assumed to include a deceased character
        actor's unlicensed archival footage — the sentence a director's
        statement crediting "Marlowe Vance" as a performer conspicuously
        never writes.
      </p>
      <TellReveal summary="What's the tell?">
        <p>
          The blink: a cadence running about four frames too slow and too
          even to be a real eyelid — the same too-smooth uncanniness this
          course named in AI voiceover back in week one, relocated to a face.
          The fictional Tri-State Automated Media Festival's disclosure rule
          checks a submission for the phrase "synthetic performer" or
          "synthetic likeness" and nothing else, so a statement can satisfy it
          by keyword while its own prose still describes Marlowe Vance as if
          cast.
        </p>
      </TellReveal>
    </article>
  </div>

  <p>
    Nightshade &amp; Nectar's seed drift and Marlowe Vance turn up once more,
    side by side, in{" "}
    <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-12/")}>Week 12's retrospective</a>. One
    is a coherence failure anyone could point at on a bad day. The other is a
    case this course kept coming back to, because the craft had gotten good
    enough to make the ethics harder, not easier.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .specimens {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(18rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--at-spacing-xl, 2rem);
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xl, 2rem);
  }

  .specimen {
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border, currentColor);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius, 8px);
    padding: var(--at-spacing-lg, 1.25rem);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .specimen h2 {
    margin-block-start: var(--at-spacing-md, 1rem);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-h4, 1.15rem);
  }

  .specimen-media {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(9rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--at-spacing-sm, 0.75rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .specimen-still {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .specimen-photo {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .specimen-photo :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 2 / 3;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius, 8px);
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border, currentColor);
  }

  .specimen-photo figcaption {
    margin-block-start: var(--at-spacing-sm, 0.4rem);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .specimen-tag {
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    margin-block-end: var(--at-spacing-sm, 0.75rem);
  }
</style>
